Shows every pin of the board's chip: which driver or program holds it (or that the board wires
it to something fixed), what the IO_MUX and the GPIO matrix route through it, its direction and
pulls, and its level, live, ten times a second while the window is shown.

Pins lists them all, sortable, by class or by connector; choose one for everything about it
(its IO_MUX functions, the matrix signals in and out, drive, strapping). Board draws the board's
connectors (UEXT, EXT1) as their headers are laid out, each pin coloured by who holds it with
its level inside, and then every pad of the chip; click one to see it on Pins.

Beside a pin, its setup: input, output or open drain, its level and pulls, its edges watched
and drawn; a pin a driver holds is asked about before it is taken. Ports lists BMDS's UART and
SPI ports and the system's I2C buses: a port's pins and settings as fields, started, stopped and
saved, and a tool for each: a terminal on a UART, a scan and register reads and writes on an
I2C bus, transfers on an SPI device.

It reads /dev/gpiochip0, the same device Linux's GPIO tools use; settings go through sysconf
(io/gpio, io/uartN, io/spiN), so they last until a restart unless saved.

Needs a BMDS newer than 0.4.0 (gpio_ctl; the Ports tab: io/uartN, i2c_ctl, io/spiN).
